{"bill":{"id":"119-hr-1687","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"1687","title":"CLEAN Act","introducedDate":"2025-02-27","latestActionDate":"2026-07-15","latestActionText":"Committee on Energy and Natural Resources Subcommittee on Public Lands, Forests, and Mining. Hearings held.","policyArea":"Energy","subjects":["Alternative and renewable resources","Electric power generation and transmission","Land transfers","Mining"],"sponsorBioguideId":"F000469","cosponsorBioguideIds":["M001228","B000825","L000590","B001323"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Committing Leases for Energy Access Now Act or the CLEAN Act This bill directs the Department of the Interior to increase the frequency of lease sales for developing and utilizing geothermal energy on federal land. Specifically, Interior must hold lease sales at least once a year (rather than two years) in states with pending nominations of federal land to be leased for geothermal energy development. In conducting such lease sales, Interior must offer all of the pending nominated parcels eligible for geothermal development and utilization under the resource management plan in effect for the state. If a lease sale is canceled or delayed, Interior must conduct a replacement sale during the same year. Finally, the bill establishes deadlines for Interior to respond to applications for geothermal drilling permits.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1687","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hr/BILLSTATUS-119hr1687.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:29:51.836Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hr-1687","plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of the Interior to hold lease sales for geothermal energy development on federal land at least once a year in states where parcels have been nominated, instead of the current requirement of once every two years. It would also require Interior to offer all eligible nominated parcels at each sale and to hold a replacement sale in the same year if a sale is canceled or delayed. The bill sets deadlines for Interior to respond to applications for geothermal drilling permits.","industries":[{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects geothermal energy developers by increasing lease sale frequency and establishing permit response deadlines."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Electric utilities that generate power from geothermal sources would benefit from increased access to federal leases for development."},{"industry":"mining","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"The bill involves leasing and drilling on federal land, activities that overlap with mining sector operations and regulatory frameworks."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"efcd12afe9c8857b814e8559","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:30:36.321Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would order the Interior Department to increase the frequency of lease sales for developing and using geothermal energy on federal land.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who pays an electricity bill. People who live near a mine, and people who work in one.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in your electricity bill. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hr-1687","bioguideId":"F000469","cycle":2026,"score":0.008494547248148583,"matches":[{"industry":"utilities-electric","donorAmount":10500,"donorShare":0.017276072443869753,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.00562476777242271},{"industry":"mining","donorAmount":7500,"donorShare":0.012340051745621252,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0028697794757258726}],"method":{"f